      ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nConverting the first line to slope-intercept form gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-6y & =-4 \\\\ -6y & =-2x-4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the second line to slope-intercept form gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx+5y & =3 \\\\ 5y & =-kx+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{5}x+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{k}{5})=-1\\,\u27f9\\,k=15.\n$$",
